With regard to the etiology in
our case,a paraneoplastic process
that can be associated with the stiff
limb syndrome seems to be un-
likely considering the long dura-
tion of the symptoms without any
signs of an underlying malignancy
[8,10].Diabetes mellitus or other
autoimmune conditions that can
also be associated with this syn-
drome were excluded [2].Further-
more,there was no evidence for
cerebral or spinal structural ab-
normalities or vascular lesions and
no signs of a toxic or infectious
aetiology.A progressive encephalo-
myelitis with rigidity seems un-
likely since there were no signs of
dysfunction of descending motor
tracts or the brain stem [4].
Recently,there have been a
number of reports describing cases
of stiff limb syndromes and it
seems that the clinical appearance
of the stiff limb syndrome is rather
heterogeneous (Table 1).Thus,it
has been suggested that the stiff
limb syndrome may overlap with
the stiff man syndrome [1] or that
it represents a focal form of the
stiff man syndrome [8].
The pathogenetic mechanisms
of the stiff man and stiff limb syn-
dromes still remain unclear.An au-
toimmune disturbance of in-
hibitory GABA-ergic pathways due
to a chronic spinal interneuronitis
has been suggested [4].Further
support pointing to a dysregulation
in GABA-ergic transmission comes
from the findings of GAD-Ab as
well as from positive responses to
application of GABA-ergic drugs
and immunoglobulines [7,11].Fur-
thermore,plasma exchange might
be helpful in some cases [5] and a
paraneoplastic etiology has been
described [10].The immunological
profile,however,may be very het-
erogenous [4],but it seems that in
contrast to the stiff man syndrome
the incidence of GAD-Ab and other
pathological autoimmune findings
is lower in the stiff limb syndrome
[1].Furthermore,patients respond
less favourably to GABA-ergic
drugs than in the stiff man syn-
drome [1,7].Patients with a stiff
limb syndrome show also distinc-
tive electrophysiological findings
with abnormally segmented elec-
tromyographic discharges during
spasms and continuous motor unit
activity in the affected limb [1,6].
Therefore,in view of the hetero-
geneity of the cases described so
far,it is important to further char-
acterize the stiff limb syndrome,as
in our case,in order to refine the
present diagnostic criteria as this
might have consequences for the
clinical management.
